y're highly  portable super convenient that's good  for on the go and then at home some  glass containers investing in a good  set's a great idea our top pick is from  truseal and the nice thing about those  they can go from the fridge to the  freezer to the oven  up to 425 degrees fahrenheit so really  versatile and i don't know about you  growing up we had a million containers  and where are the lids all mismatched  you know so investments  exactly invest in a good set it's worth  it all right so you say something that's  interesting you say freezers are an ally  in helping cut down on food waste but i  feel like if you look at my fridge we  have meat in there from like the  jurassic era  you know it's been in there since like  forever meaning we don't waste a lot of  food it all gets shoved you don't even  see the light when you turn on the you  don't even know what it is you're like  what  is this and it's covered with ice burn  right well it's a good idea at least  once a week to really take inventory of  what's in the fridge and freezer  especially in the freezer even with the  best intentions things can get lost in  the back but your freezer is the  ultimate tool for reducing food waste i  want you to think anytime something is  about to turn can i freeze it yes and  you know there's a few different hacks  with that i know gail before you were  asking me about bananas bananas yeah so  that's a big one i think we're all we've  all seen it all the bananas over ripe on  the on the table at the end of the week  right so i want you to peel them you're  going to slice them and then put them on  a baking sheet or a plate lined with  parchment paper you want to freeze it in  those slices if you just throw them all  in a bag it's going to get into a big  clump yeah you won't be able to use it  what's the point right yeah once they're  frozen then you can put it in some sort  of food airtight container or in a bag  another little hack is for herbs you  know we'll buy let's say a big thing of  parsley you only use about a tablespoon  or two yeah you need a little tiny bit  yeah a huge like a thing of parsley and  then you're stuck and it ultimately goes  in the trash well i want you to cut up  those herbs put them in an ice cube tray  with olive oil then you pop them out put  them on the skillet oh okay  that's your flavor bomb that'll work  zephyr  open up a can what about expiration  dates like i have eggs right that's a  good march 29th i should buy march 29th  i know it's bad well girl what about the  hats in a little bit i don't i don't  cook but it was a while ago dates you  can use it past the expiration date or  yes  a lot of times you'll see a date on  there and you assume it's an expiration  date and you have to throw it out  immediately well typically these dates  are best by date sell by dates or use by  dates they're more indicative of quality  and flavor not necessarily that they've  gone bad so signs of spoilage you want  to look for a change in color a weird  texture an unpleasant odor exactly and  the obvious things you know molds a  bulging lid things of that nature can  also be a sign but the usda food safety  and inspection service says most of  these things are perfectly okay to  consume after the date not march 29th  though but i think that's a little  we'll be right back
 

